00:00.500 --> 00:03.419
In this lesson, you'll be taking what you've learned so far

00:03.419 --> 00:08.009
about getting structured responses from an LLM using Pydantic data models.

00:08.009 --> 00:11.210
and you'll combine that with a new ability

00:11.210 --> 00:13.859
to use Pydantic models in the definition of tools

00:13.859 --> 00:16.350
that you can pass in your LLM API call.

00:16.350 --> 00:19.100
And so, when it comes to the ways in which

00:19.100 --> 00:22.989
Pydantic gets used in LLM workflows, these are the two big ones.

00:22.989 --> 00:25.750
structured responses and tool calling.

00:25.750 --> 00:28.250
And so what you'll do in this lesson

00:28.250 --> 00:29.798
is combine these to implement

00:29.798 --> 00:32.618
reliable structure and tool calling in your application.

00:33.128 --> 00:36.609
So, let's jump into the code. There's going to be a lot

00:36.609 --> 00:38.149
of code flying by in this lesson.

00:38.149 --> 00:40.253
So I first want to give you just

00:40.253 --> 00:42.359
a high-level visual picture for where we're going,

00:42.359 --> 00:44.658
and hopefully that'll make it a little easier to follow along.

00:45.018 --> 00:46.598
So, the starting point is the same.

00:46.838 --> 00:50.639
Namely, you're getting some user input that includes a name, an email,

00:50.639 --> 00:53.149
and some kind of message like what's my order status?

00:53.749 --> 00:56.218
You'll first take that user input

00:56.218 --> 00:59.598
and validate it using your UserInput model. Then you'll

00:59.598 --> 01:03.329
pass your validated user input into an LLM call

01:03.329 --> 01:06.069
where you're asking the LLM to return

01:06.069 --> 01:08.808
a valid instance of your CustomerQuery model.

01:08.808 --> 01:11.644
And then you'll pass that to another

01:11.644 --> 01:14.478
LLM API call with some tool definitions.

01:14.478 --> 01:17.718
And so the way you're going to code that up

01:17.718 --> 01:20.959
is first to start with two new pydantic data models.

01:20.959 --> 01:24.638
And these are FAQLookupArgs and CheckOrderStatusArgs.

01:24.638 --> 01:27.599
and you'll see how those get implemented in the notebook.

01:27.599 --> 01:29.399
But basically these are

01:29.399 --> 01:32.299
finding the parameters that you'll use to make a tool call.

01:32.299 --> 01:36.338
And those parameters go into two new functions that you'll define.

01:36.338 --> 01:41.358
So you'll have lookup_faq_answer that takes FAQLookupArgs as input,

01:41.358 --> 01:45.429
and check_order_status that takes CheckOrderStatusArgs as input.

01:45.429 --> 01:46.938
So you'll see how those functions

01:46.938 --> 01:48.698
get defined in the notebook as well.

01:48.698 --> 01:51.198
After that, you can make some tool_definitions

01:51.198 --> 01:54.203
where you'll define tools that include a name

01:54.203 --> 01:57.209
that's the Python function for that tool call,

01:57.209 --> 01:59.198
and a description, and then the parameters.

01:59.198 --> 02:02.069
and for parameters you're going to pass in the model_json_schema

02:02.069 --> 02:05.328
of those pydantic data models that you defined above.

02:05.328 --> 02:07.358
And then you'll take those tool definitions

02:07.358 --> 02:09.788
and then pass them in the API call.

02:09.788 --> 02:13.109
And that's letting the LLM know which tools are available

02:13.109 --> 02:16.377
and then the job of the LLM is to return parameters for

02:16.377 --> 02:18.760
a tool call if in fact that seems like

02:18.760 --> 02:21.408
a good next step. And then you'll take that response

02:21.408 --> 02:26.429
and first use the pydantic data models that you defined for FAQLookupArgs

02:26.429 --> 02:29.048
or CheckOrderStatusArgs and

02:29.048 --> 02:30.844
validate that the tool parameters returned by

02:30.844 --> 02:32.897
the LLM are what they need to be

02:32.897 --> 02:34.591
in order to call those functions, and

02:34.591 --> 02:36.527
then you'll go ahead and call those tools,

02:36.527 --> 02:38.427
return the outputs,

02:38.427 --> 02:40.828
and then you'll take all of that and

02:40.828 --> 02:44.027
bundle it up into one more LLM call where you're passing

02:44.027 --> 02:47.228
in your customer query, your tool outputs,

02:47.228 --> 02:49.027
and you've defined another

02:49.027 --> 02:51.877
pydantic data model called SupportTicket, which will be the final

02:51.877 --> 02:54.127
output from that last LLM call.

02:54.127 --> 02:55.652
So, you're going to be putting together a

02:55.652 --> 02:57.177
lot of different pieces and this is the

02:57.177 --> 02:59.737
big picture. All right, so

03:00.048 --> 03:02.842
in this notebook, there's going to be a fair amount of setup

03:02.842 --> 03:05.638
and a fair amount of code flying by, so bear with me.

03:05.638 --> 03:08.737
I'll try to move quickly through the parts that you've seen before

03:08.737 --> 03:11.858
and pause to explain each time there's something new.

03:11.858 --> 03:14.828
And so the first thing is just a whole bunch of imports.

03:14.828 --> 03:17.068
This is basically everything you've seen from the previous lessons.

03:17.068 --> 03:18.948
Now you're going to bring it all together.

03:18.948 --> 03:22.737
And next, you're going to define your UserInput data model.

03:22.737 --> 03:25.777
But in this case, you're making it a little bit more complex

03:25.777 --> 03:28.358
by messing with the order_id field.

03:28.598 --> 03:30.228
So order_id, which used to be an integer,

03:30.228 --> 03:32.418
is now going to be an optional string field,

03:32.418 --> 03:34.078
where the default is still None.

03:34.078 --> 03:36.837
And the description is that there's a new format.

03:36.837 --> 03:40.497
You have three capital letters, then a dash, and then five numbers.

03:40.497 --> 03:43.647
And the reason this is an interesting example is just because

03:43.647 --> 03:48.647
often times you'll have fields like this where validation is not straightforward.

03:48.647 --> 03:50.698
And that's where you need to bring in this field_validator

03:50.698 --> 03:52.427
tool from pydantic.

03:52.427 --> 03:55.098
So with field_validator, you can set

03:55.098 --> 03:58.397
your validator on the order_id field and then define a

03:58.397 --> 04:00.403
function, that in this case just has

04:00.403 --> 04:02.698
some regular expression parsing to see if the

04:02.698 --> 04:05.698
pattern in the input matches the expectation.

04:05.698 --> 04:08.198
And if it doesn't match, you can raise a ValueError,

04:08.198 --> 04:10.147
letting the user know what the problem

04:10.147 --> 04:12.098
is and what the expected format is.

04:12.098 --> 04:14.698
And so, this is just one example of how you might

04:14.698 --> 04:17.697
handle some custom validation directly within your model.

04:17.697 --> 04:19.358
And it's a way in which

04:19.358 --> 04:21.298
you can enforce security on input data.

04:21.298 --> 04:23.963
For example, if you had input coming in and you

04:23.963 --> 04:26.898
wanted to make sure it's not some kind of SQL injection

04:26.898 --> 04:30.367
or some kind of other malicious input, you can validate whatever

04:30.367 --> 04:32.769
you've got coming into your data model

04:32.769 --> 04:35.160
with completely custom validation logic like this.

04:35.160 --> 04:36.850
So you can define that model,

04:37.069 --> 04:40.829
and then you're going to define your customer query data model,

04:40.829 --> 04:42.480
just as you did before,

04:42.480 --> 04:46.139
inheriting from UserInput and with these four additional fields.

04:47.980 --> 04:51.279
Next, you're going to define a validate_user_input function,

04:51.279 --> 04:54.430
just as you did before, so I won't say much about that.

04:55.620 --> 04:57.997
And then you'll define a

04:57.997 --> 04:59.997
create customer_query function

04:59.997 --> 05:01.726
that takes in validated user data

05:01.726 --> 05:03.747
in the form of a JSON string

05:03.747 --> 05:09.197
and calls an LLM to populate an instance of CustomerQuery.

05:09.197 --> 05:10.812
So this is just a slight

05:10.812 --> 05:12.697
twist on what you were doing before.

05:12.697 --> 05:15.096
Essentially the same thing, you're just calling an LLM

05:15.096 --> 05:17.896
using in this case output_type of CustomerQuery.

05:17.896 --> 05:21.896
Now here we're using the Pydantic AI framework and Google's Gemini model,

05:21.896 --> 05:24.497
but this is just the same thing you were doing before,

05:24.497 --> 05:26.172
calling an LLM to take user input

05:26.172 --> 05:29.047
and populate an instance of the class. a customer query model.

05:29.266 --> 05:31.526
And so in this case you're returning the response.output,

05:31.526 --> 05:34.209
which when you're calling Pydantic AI is

05:34.209 --> 05:37.276
the validated instance of your CustomerQuery data model.

05:39.307 --> 05:41.007
And then you can try out what you've got so far.

05:41.507 --> 05:43.247
So here you have some user input

05:43.247 --> 05:44.940
in the form of a JSON

05:44.940 --> 05:46.916
string. You can validate the user input,

05:47.177 --> 05:50.177
then create a customer query and print out what you've got.

05:52.637 --> 05:54.846
All right, user input validated, CustomerQuery generated

05:54.846 --> 05:57.596
and looks like you got a valid instance of your customer

05:57.596 --> 05:59.747
query data model out the other end.

06:00.607 --> 06:03.726
And after that, you're going to define some new pydantic models.

06:03.956 --> 06:06.867
In this first case, you'll define a Pydantic

06:06.867 --> 06:09.776
model called FAQLookupArgs that inherits from BaseModel and

06:10.466 --> 06:12.817
has these two fields, query and tags.

06:13.297 --> 06:16.987
And so these two fields are what will be expected in

06:16.987 --> 06:20.677
an FAQ lookup function that you'll define in just a moment.

06:23.026 --> 06:27.396
And then you'll define another model called CheckOrderStatusArgs that

06:27.396 --> 06:29.252
also inherits from BaseModel and has

06:29.252 --> 06:31.416
an order_id field, just like what you

06:31.416 --> 06:33.947
had before with this field_validator on the order_id field,

06:33.947 --> 06:37.802
and an email. And so these are going to be the parameters

06:37.802 --> 06:39.507
that go passed into a check

06:39.507 --> 06:41.497
order status function. So let's define that.

06:43.076 --> 06:47.447
And then you're also going to define some fake database data.

06:47.447 --> 06:50.872
So in this case, just some data that you can actually look

06:50.872 --> 06:54.297
up with an FAQ lookup function or an order status lookup function.

06:54.297 --> 06:58.045
And so here you have a tiny little fake FAQ database with

06:58.045 --> 07:01.735
a question, answer, and keyword for each FAQ.

07:01.735 --> 07:04.235
And down here you have a a little order database

07:04.235 --> 07:06.367
with three different orders including a

07:06.367 --> 07:08.855
status, estimated delivery, purchase date, and email.

07:08.855 --> 07:10.175
And so you don't need to worry

07:10.175 --> 07:11.495
about the details of what's in here.

07:11.495 --> 07:13.975
This is just in order to complete the example.

07:15.425 --> 07:19.446
And then you can define the lookup_faq_answer function,

07:19.446 --> 07:22.995
which in this case takes as input an instance

07:22.995 --> 07:26.656
of the FAQLookUpArgs model that you created above.

07:27.415 --> 07:29.675
And this is just a function to go through

07:29.675 --> 07:33.776
that fake database of FAQs and search based on

07:33.776 --> 07:35.935
keywords in the user input.

07:36.206 --> 07:37.966
And if it finds an answer, it returns the answer.

07:38.225 --> 07:39.571
If not, it says, sorry, I couldn't

07:39.571 --> 07:40.915
find an FAQ answer for your question.

07:43.656 --> 07:47.005
And then you can define a check_order_status function,

07:47.005 --> 07:51.805
which in this case takes as input the CheckOrderStatusArgs pydantic model,

07:51.805 --> 07:54.906
a valid instance of that model as input,

07:54.906 --> 07:56.875
and then attempts to look up an order,

07:56.875 --> 07:58.625
from that little fake order database

07:58.625 --> 08:00.985
using the order ID and email

08:00.985 --> 08:02.495
for confirmation of

08:02.495 --> 08:04.216
a matching order.

08:04.216 --> 08:06.946
Again, the details here are not something you need to worry about.

08:06.946 --> 08:08.271
This is all just in service of

08:08.271 --> 08:09.596
building up an example of tool calling

08:09.596 --> 08:11.565
in your customer support system.

08:13.115 --> 08:14.855
And then finally we get to the interesting part.

08:15.786 --> 08:17.526
You can define tools

08:17.526 --> 08:19.786
that you'll use in your API call

08:19.786 --> 08:22.855
to an LLM like this, where up here you have

08:22.855 --> 08:26.375
a first tool that has a name of lookup_faq_answer,

08:26.596 --> 08:28.545
answer, that's the, that's the function that you'll

08:28.545 --> 08:30.495
call if in fact this tool is invoked.

08:30.935 --> 08:34.596
And the parameters that that function expects are the FAQLookupArgs.

08:35.405 --> 08:37.785
And so you're passing this information in your API

08:37.785 --> 08:40.166
call to an LLM, and this is where your

08:40.505 --> 08:42.412
Pydantic data model comes in in

08:42.412 --> 08:44.635
defining the parameters that that tool expects.

08:45.105 --> 08:48.258
And then you have another one for check_order_status where the

08:48.258 --> 08:51.725
parameters are the schema of that check order status args model.

08:53.436 --> 08:56.394
And as a final step, before getting into the LLM calls,

08:56.394 --> 09:00.144
you're going to define a couple more pydantic models.

09:00.144 --> 09:02.649
So this is now going to be your SupportTicket

09:02.649 --> 09:05.154
model, which is the ultimate output of the system.

09:05.154 --> 09:08.105
And so first you've got an OrderDetails model that

09:08.105 --> 09:11.384
just has a few fields of status, estimated_delivery, and note.

09:11.384 --> 09:13.460
And then what you're going to do is

09:13.460 --> 09:15.534
you're going to use OrderDetails inside of SupportTicket.

09:15.534 --> 09:19.235
So now SupportTicket is a model that inherits from CustomerQuery,

09:19.235 --> 09:20.961
so it has all the attributes of

09:20.961 --> 09:22.934
CustomerQuery. And then you're adding some new things.

09:22.934 --> 09:26.284
So a recommended_next_action, which is another Literal that

09:26.284 --> 09:30.325
has a certain set of acceptable values, escalate_to_agent, send_faq_response,

09:30.325 --> 09:32.554
send_order_status or no_action_needed.

09:32.554 --> 09:34.601
You have an order_details field and this is

09:34.601 --> 09:36.904
where you're going to pull in that OrderDetails model.

09:36.904 --> 09:38.828
And so, this is an example

09:38.828 --> 09:41.075
of how you can construct a field

09:41.075 --> 09:45.715
inside of a Pydantic data model using another Pydantic data model.

09:46.014 --> 09:48.475
And then you have an faq_response that can take

09:48.475 --> 09:50.934
the response if you looked up an FAQ answer

09:50.934 --> 09:53.894
and a creation_date. So let's define those.

09:54.615 --> 09:56.174
And now with all that infrastructure

09:56.195 --> 09:59.794
built up, you're ready to define your LLM call.

10:00.804 --> 10:06.144
And so what you're going to do is define this decide_next_action_with_tools function.

10:06.695 --> 10:10.784
And this takes as input an instance of your CustomerQuery data model.

10:11.144 --> 10:12.519
And so the first thing you're going

10:12.519 --> 10:13.894
to do is define a system prompt.

10:14.284 --> 10:17.054
And to do that, you'll first extract the model_json_schema

10:17.804 --> 10:19.695
from the SupportTicket model.

10:20.075 --> 10:21.344
and then construct a prompt that

10:21.344 --> 10:22.825
says you're a helpful customer support agent.

10:23.174 --> 10:24.509
Your job is to determine what support

10:24.509 --> 10:25.845
action should be taken for the customer,

10:26.087 --> 10:28.668
based on the query and expected fields in the SupportTicket schema below.

10:29.222 --> 10:30.893
And so the idea here

10:30.893 --> 10:33.623
is that there's input from the user.

10:33.623 --> 10:35.893
It gets converted via a first LLM call

10:35.893 --> 10:38.413
into a customer query data model.

10:38.413 --> 10:40.682
and then this is passed on to another LLM call

10:40.682 --> 10:43.442
to decide what to do, to call a tool

10:43.442 --> 10:46.852
or to construct a support ticket of a different form.

10:46.852 --> 10:49.163
So it says if more information on a particular order_id

10:49.163 --> 10:52.342
or FAQ response would be helpful in responding to the user query

10:52.342 --> 10:53.942
and can be obtained by calling a tool,

10:53.942 --> 10:55.952
then call the appropriate tool to get that information.

10:56.744 --> 10:58.465
And then here at the bottom

10:58.465 --> 11:00.474
you're passing in the support_ticket_schema as the

11:00.474 --> 11:02.504
expectation for where you're ultimately trying to go.

11:02.803 --> 11:04.613
Now this is not saying, give

11:04.613 --> 11:06.724
me back this structure in the response,

11:06.823 --> 11:08.763
because what you're going to do with this LLM

11:08.763 --> 11:10.703
call is decide whether or not to call tools.

11:10.803 --> 11:14.504
But this helps the LLM decide whether or not a tool call

11:14.504 --> 11:16.796
is a good idea in this case. So in

11:16.796 --> 11:19.344
this case your system_prompt is all this stuff up here,

11:19.543 --> 11:22.484
and then your user prompt is the model_dump

11:22.484 --> 11:24.943
of the customer_query model that you passed in.

11:25.744 --> 11:28.313
So this is all the information that was in the user input

11:28.864 --> 11:31.403
as well as the additional information from the first LLM call

11:31.663 --> 11:33.793
when you populated the customer query model.

11:34.094 --> 11:37.344
And then in this case you're going to call OpenAI, gpt-4o

11:37.833 --> 11:41.323
and you're passing in those tool definitions in the tools parameter,

11:41.683 --> 11:43.532
which lets the LLM know what tools

11:43.532 --> 11:45.644
are available to call as a next step.

11:45.874 --> 11:47.946
So let's go ahead and define

11:47.946 --> 11:50.364
that. And now you're ready for action.

11:50.953 --> 11:55.224
So you can call the decide_next_action_with_tools function, pass

11:55.573 --> 11:57.788
in customer_query. And what you're going to

11:57.788 --> 12:00.004
get back remember is the message response

12:00.004 --> 12:03.163
from the LLM, the tool_calls if there were any

12:03.163 --> 12:04.908
that were extracted from that message,

12:04.908 --> 12:06.943
and then the input prompts as well.

12:06.943 --> 12:09.994
And then with these print statements are doing are just printing out

12:09.994 --> 12:13.043
those tool_calls if in fact the LLM is suggesting a tool call.

12:13.464 --> 12:14.784
So you can run that. And so

12:14.784 --> 12:16.104
what you get back in this case

12:16.104 --> 12:17.846
is that the content of the message,

12:17.846 --> 12:19.589
that place where you were previously getting

12:19.589 --> 12:21.331
back a free form text response from

12:21.331 --> 12:23.323
the LLM, that's just null in this case,

12:23.323 --> 12:24.671
but down here in tool_calls, you

12:24.671 --> 12:26.244
can see that there's an indication to

12:26.244 --> 12:28.943
call the check_order_status tool.

12:28.943 --> 12:30.239
And so you can print that out

12:30.239 --> 12:31.533
a little bit more cleanly down here.

12:31.533 --> 12:33.063
tool_calls is passing in an order_id

12:33.063 --> 12:37.173
and an email and wants to call the check_order_status function.

12:37.173 --> 12:38.903
And so the LLM is saying,

12:38.903 --> 12:41.884
go ahead and call the check_order_status function with these parameters.

12:42.374 --> 12:43.724
So the next thing you're going

12:43.724 --> 12:45.298
to do is set up a function

12:45.298 --> 12:46.649
to call tools if in fact

12:46.649 --> 12:48.224
the LLM wants to call a tool.

12:48.504 --> 12:51.649
And so you can define a function called

12:51.649 --> 12:54.793
get_tool_outputs, passing in tool_calls from the LLM response.

12:54.793 --> 12:56.413
and then if there was a tool call,

12:56.693 --> 12:59.673
go ahead and call the lookup_faq_answer function

12:59.673 --> 13:02.724
if that's the tool indicated, or the check_order_status

13:03.364 --> 13:06.514
function if that was the tool that the LLM indicated.

13:06.953 --> 13:08.274
And what you're doing as a first

13:08.274 --> 13:09.783
step in the event of a tool call

13:10.403 --> 13:13.433
is passing in the tool_call function arguments

13:14.124 --> 13:17.874
into your FAQLookupArgs pydantic data model in this case

13:18.104 --> 13:21.474
and using the model_validate_json method to validate whether

13:21.833 --> 13:24.592
the parameters passed by the LLM are in

13:24.592 --> 13:26.352
fact valid for that tool call.

13:26.602 --> 13:28.407
So your pydantic data model came

13:28.407 --> 13:30.513
into play in the API call itself

13:30.513 --> 13:32.389
as part of the tool definition for

13:32.389 --> 13:34.533
the LLM to know what parameters are valid.

13:34.743 --> 13:36.995
And then the first thing you do when you get

13:36.995 --> 13:39.472
back parameters from the LLM is use that model to validate

13:39.472 --> 13:41.732
that those parameters returned by the LLM are going to work.

13:41.732 --> 13:44.246
And then the next step is to go ahead and

13:44.246 --> 13:47.013
call that function, passing in those arguments and return the results.

13:47.342 --> 13:49.633
And then the same thing for check_order_status.

13:49.633 --> 13:51.759
The first thing to do is validate that

13:51.759 --> 13:54.153
the parameters returned by the LLM are in fact

13:54.692 --> 13:57.153
matching the expectations of your model

13:57.153 --> 13:58.932
and then call that function.

13:58.932 --> 14:01.413
And in either case you'll return the outputs of the function.

14:01.413 --> 14:05.602
So you can run this cell to define the get_tool_outputs function

14:05.602 --> 14:07.927
and use it on the tool calls that

14:07.927 --> 14:10.253
you got back in the last LLM response.

14:12.232 --> 14:14.600
And so you can see that you've got the

14:14.600 --> 14:17.232
agent requested a call to the Check Order Status tool.

